faq:
  - q: "Does my child need a diagnosis or a referral?"
    a: "No. If something is making daily life harder, that's enough to start with. You don't need a diagnosis, a referral, or a waiting list place — just a conversation."
  - q: "Do you take insurance?"
    a: "I'm a private-pay practice, at $150 an hour. That means no diagnosis requirement, no cap on sessions, no waiting on authorizations, and no pressure to fit your child into a billing category. It also means the cost sits with your family rather than an insurer, which is a real trade-off and worth talking through before you commit to anything. Call or email me and we'll get into it."
  - q: "What ages do you work with?"
    a: "<p>Birth to twelve. Much of my background is with babies and toddlers, birth to three, and that's where I'm most specialized. I also work with older children on play, social skills, fine motor development and daily routines.</p><p>If your child is older than twelve, ask anyway — I take teenagers up to 18 by arrangement, depending on what's going on and whether I'm genuinely the right person. Calling me is the fastest way to find out.</p>"
  - q: "What area do you cover?"
    a: "In person, Port Townsend and East Jefferson County — in your home or out in the community. Remotely, anywhere on the Olympic Peninsula. <a href=\"/in-home-ot/\">More about how sessions work →</a>"
  - q: "We're already working with early intervention or the school. Does this conflict?"
    a: "Not at all. I'm happy to work alongside what's already in place, and to coordinate with your other providers if you'd like. School-based OT is limited to what affects a child's access to education; private OT isn't, so families often do both."
---

