<div class="split-body">
<p class="lead">A child's "occupations" are simply the things they do all day — <span class="occ">eating, sleeping, dressing, playing, learning, being with other people</span>. Pediatric occupational therapy helps when some of those things have become hard, by working out what's getting in the way and changing either the skill, the activity, or the environment around it.</p>
<p class="big">It isn't about jobs, and it isn't only for children with a diagnosis. Much of the time it looks like play, because for a young child play <em>is</em> the work.</p>
</div>
