<section class="sec sec-alt tint-green" id="help" aria-labelledby="help-h">
<div class="container">
<div class="sec-head">
<h2 id="help-h">“Is this even an OT thing?”</h2>
<p>It's the question I get most. Occupational therapy looks at how a child and their environment fit together — and where that fit could be easier. If any of these sound familiar, it's worth a conversation.</p>
</div>
<div class="grid2">
<div class="card">
<span class="chip chip-red" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 46 46"><path d="M23 3c11 0 20 8 20 20s-9 20-20 20S3 34 3 23 12 3 23 3Z"/></svg></span>
<h3>Mealtimes have become a battle.</h3>
<p>Very limited foods, distress at the table, or a child who can't seem to sit through a meal. We look at the sensory side, the seating, the routine, and the pressure — and make eating feel possible again.</p>
</div>
<div class="card">
<span class="chip chip-yellow" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 46 46"><path d="M23 4 43 40H3Z"/></svg></span>
<h3>Getting dressed, bathed, or out the door takes everything you have.</h3>
<p>We break down where the routine is actually breaking, adjust what we can, and build the specific skills underneath it — rather than pushing harder on a routine that isn't working.</p>
</div>
<div class="card">
<span class="chip chip-blue" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 46 46"><path d="M23 3 43 23 23 43 3 23Z"/></svg></span>
<h3>Big feelings arrive faster than either of you can manage.</h3>
<p>We work out what's setting things off and what your child is trying to communicate, then build tools and adjust the environment so those moments come less often and land more softly.</p>
</div>
<div class="card">
<span class="chip chip-green" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 46 46"><path d="M3 23c0-11 9-20 20-20s20 9 20 20v20H23C12 43 3 34 3 23Z"/></svg></span>
<h3>Sounds, textures, clothing, or movement seem to affect your child more than most.</h3>
<p>Sensory processing shapes a huge amount of daily life. We figure out your child's particular sensory picture and what genuinely helps them feel regulated.</p>
</div>
<div class="card">
<span class="chip chip-pink" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 46 46"><path d="M3 23a20 20 0 0 1 40 0 20 20 0 0 1-40 0Z"/><path class="cut" d="M21.5 3h3v40h-3Z"/></svg></span>
<h3>You're not sure how to play together, or how to help them play with others.</h3>
<p>For babies and toddlers especially, play is where everything else grows from. We build foundational play and communication, and — as they get older — the social skills that let them join in.</p>
</div>
<div class="card">
<span class="chip chip-yellow" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 46 46"><path d="M6 6h28v28H6Z"/><path class="cut" d="M14 14h20v20H14Z"/><path d="M17 17h20v20H17Z"/></svg></span>
<h3>Small hands are struggling with everyday tools.</h3>
<p>Fine motor development affects everything from spoons to crayons to buttons. We work on it inside real activities, not drills.</p>
</div>
</div>
